{"id":"n-20261030-kcvyi3","category":"health","display_title":"Police Shift Mental Health Policy","display_title_he":"המשטרה משנה את מדיניות בריאות הנפש","short_summary":"Northern Ireland police are shifting how they handle mental health calls, sparking both support and resource concerns.","english_level":"C1","words":495,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvglwd7y00g5o","title":"How a major policy shift is changing how police will respond to mental health call-outs","published_at":"2026-10-30T06:16:08.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-30T06:16:08.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-30T07:53:20.937Z","quality_score":10,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A major policy shift is transforming how police in Northern Ireland respond to mental health call-outs. According to officials, the Police Service of Northern Ireland handles approximately 30,000 mental health calls every year, averaging about 80 calls daily. However, under a new policy initiative starting from the beginning of November, the PSNI will discontinue routine concern-for-welfare checks unless there is an active crime, a risk of harm, or a direct risk to life.","While the public will continue to dial 999 in emergencies, call handlers will evaluate the situations and may transfer callers to alternative emergency services or health and social care staff. Assistant Chief Constable Ryan Henderson previously reported to a Stormont committee that the volume of mental health contacts had reached unprecedented levels, noting that merely 3% of those potential danger calls involved actual criminal activity. A significant proportion of these calls actually come from health workers themselves. This phased transition mirrors the 'Right Care Right Person' framework, which has been operational among various police forces across England and Wales since 2023.","Proponents of the policy argue that uniformed officers are not always the ideal responders for individuals experiencing psychological distress. Charles Little, whose in-laws Michael and Marjorie Cawdery were tragically killed in a frenzied attack in their home in 2017 by an attacker suffering from paranoid schizophrenia, expressed support for the change. The tragic murders had prompted a review into missed opportunities by the PSNI and the Southern Health and Social Care Trust to detain the perpetrator. Little explained that encountering the police during a severe mental health crisis can sometimes frighten individuals and worsen the situation, noting that police are effectively acting as the mental health front line at the moment. Similarly, Northern Ireland's Mental Health Champion, Professor Siobhan O'Neill, described the transition as a necessary step forward, emphasizing that officers were previously attending situations that did not constitute true policing matters, though she expressed disappointment regarding the lack of investment in backfill alternative services.","Despite these endorsements, representatives from the healthcare and social work sectors have voiced serious apprehensions regarding the readiness of the system. Dr Ross Irvine from the Royal College of Psychiatrists warned that local mental health services are currently operating at a crisis point, with 30% of posts across the province sitting vacant. He cautioned that removing the safety net currently provided by the PSNI could severely strain healthcare services that are already struggling to stand still. George Hannah from the British Association of Social Workers echoed these fears, pointing out that the rollout feels too quick, that there are not sufficient resources in the system to do it safely, and that a lack of access to additional support when needed might increase concerns rather than help.","Ultimately, while the PSNI maintains that the new approach will reduce avoidable delays, improve consistency in decision-making, support safe and lawful practice, and ensure individuals receive specialized care from the most appropriate professionals, questions concerning funding and resource availability continue to cast a shadow over the transition."],"facts":[{"fact":"The Police Service of Northern Ireland (PSNI) handles approximately 30,000 mental health calls annually.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Starting in November, the PSNI will implement the 'Right Care Right Person' policy, ending routine concern-for-welfare checks unless a crime, risk of harm, or risk to life is involved.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Assistant Chief Constable Ryan Henderson stated that only 3% of potential danger calls received by the PSNI relate to crime.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Similar policies have been used in England and Wales since 2023.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Charles Little, whose in-laws Michael and Marjorie Cawdery were killed in 2017 by Thomas Scott McEntee, supports the policy, arguing that police can sometimes frighten individuals in crisis.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Dr Ross Irvine of the Royal College of Psychiatrists raised concerns that 30% of mental health posts across Northern Ireland are vacant and that health services are at crisis point.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"George Hannah from the British Association of Social Workers stated that the system lacks sufficient resources to adopt the change safely.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Professor Siobhan O'Neill, the Mental Health Champion for Northern Ireland, supports the policy as an important step forward, though she noted disappointment regarding the lack of funding for alternative services.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"major","lemma":"major","pos":"adj","he":"מרכזי","name":false},{"text":"policy","lemma":"policy","pos":"noun","he":"מדיניות","name":false},{"text":"shift","lemma":"shift","pos":"noun","he":"שינוי","name":false},{"text":"transforming","lemma":"transform","pos":"verb","he":"מְשַׁנֶּה","name":false},{"text":"police","lemma":"police","pos":"noun","he":"משטרה","name":false},{"text":"Northern Ireland","lemma":"Northern Ireland","pos":"noun","he":"צפון אירלנד","name":"true"},{"text":"respond","lemma":"respond","pos":"verb","he":"להגיב","name":false},{"text":"mental","lemma":"mental","pos":"adj","he":"נפשי","name":false},{"text":"health","lemma":"health","pos":"noun","he":"בריאות","name":false},{"text":"call-outs","lemma":"call-out","pos":"noun","he":"קריאות שירות","name":false},{"text":"officials","lemma":"official","pos":"noun","he":"פקידים / גורמים רשמיים","name":false},{"text":"Police Service of Northern Ireland","lemma":"Police Service of Northern Ireland","pos":"noun","he":"שירות המשטרה של צפון אירלנד","name":"true"},{"text":"PSNI","lemma":"PSNI","pos":"noun","he":"משטרת צפון אירלנד","name":"true"},{"text":"handles","lemma":"handle","pos":"verb","he":"מטפל ב","name":false},{"text":"approximately","lemma":"approximately","pos":"adv","he":"בקירוב","name":false},{"text":"averaging","lemma":"average","pos":"verb","he":"בממוצע","name":false},{"text":"daily","lemma":"daily","pos":"adv","he":"יומי","name":false},{"text":"initiative","lemma":"initiative","pos":"noun","he":"יוזמה","name":false},{"text":"discontinue","lemma":"discontinue","pos":"verb","he":"להפסיק","name":false},{"text":"routine","lemma":"routine","pos":"adj","he":"שגרתי","name":false},{"text":"concern-for-welfare checks","lemma":"concern-for-welfare check","pos":"noun","he":"בדיקות רווחה / דאגה לשלום אדם","name":false},{"text":"active","lemma":"active","pos":"adj","he":"פעיל","name":false},{"text":"crime","lemma":"crime","pos":"noun","he":"פשע","name":false},{"text":"risk","lemma":"risk","pos":"noun","he":"סיכון","name":false},{"text":"harm","lemma":"harm","pos":"noun","he":"פגיעה","name":false},{"text":"direct","lemma":"direct","pos":"adj","he":"ישיר","name":false},{"text":"life","lemma":"life","pos":"noun","he":"חיים","name":false},{"text":"public","lemma":"public","pos":"noun","he":"ציבור","name":false},{"text